Practical No. 20
Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) for Secured REST APIs
Spring Boot 3 + Maven + H2 Database + Spring Data JPA + Spring Security (Basic Auth)
Objective
To configure CORS at application level so that a secured REST API (HTTP Basic Auth, Notes stored in
H2) can be consumed by a browser frontend running on a different origin (http://localhost:3000), while
every other origin is rejected.
What is an origin? Scheme + host + port. The API at http://localhost:8080 and a frontend at
http://localhost:3000 are different origins (different port), so the browser's same-origin policy stops the
frontend's JavaScript from reading the API's response unless the API answers with an
Access-Control-Allow-Origin header that names the frontend. Requests with a custom header
(Authorization) or JSON body, or with PUT/DELETE, are first preceded by a preflight OPTIONS request that
carries no credentials.
