Key Points to Remember
• CORS is enforced by browsers, not by the API. It stops other websites' scripts from reading your API
responses with the user's browser; it does not replace authentication, and cURL/Postman ignore it.
• Why .cors() is needed: without it the preflight OPTIONS request (which carries no Authorization
header) reaches the authorization rules and gets 401, so the browser never sends the real request.
.cors(Customizer.withDefaults()) makes Spring Security use the rules from CorsConfig.
• Never combine allowedOrigins("*") with allowCredentials(true). Spring rejects it; list exact origins
(or use allowedOriginPatterns carefully). Origins must match scheme, host and port exactly.
• allowedHeaders must include Authorization because the frontend sends Basic Auth in that header;
Content-Type is needed for JSON bodies.
• Alternative: define a CorsConfigurationSource bean in SecurityConfig instead of CorsConfig;
.cors(Customizer.withDefaults()) picks that bean up automatically.
• Per-controller option: @CrossOrigin(origins = "http://localhost:3000") on a class or method, but the
global CorsConfig is easier to audit.
• In production replace localhost:3000 with the real HTTPS frontend origin, ideally from configuration.
Conclusion
CORS was configured globally for /api/** to allow only http://localhost:3000 for GET, POST, PUT and
DELETE, and enabled in Spring Security so preflight requests succeed before authentication. Postman
tests with an Origin header show the Access-Control-Allow-Origin header for the allowed origin on all four
verbs, and its absence (with a 403) for a disallowed origin.
